  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 AM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nCurrently, a closed low is located to our north with a frontal \nboundary along western portions of the area. Ahead of the cold \nfront, showers and storms have provided widespread rain to the area. \nFog has already been developing, and a Dense Fog Advisory has been \nissued for part of the area. The front will gradually move through \nthe area during the day with a few additional showers possible. \nOtherwise, gradually clearing conditions are expected with \nnorthwesterly winds ahead of high pressure producing seasonally \ncooler temperatures by Friday morning. As the closed low to the \nnorth approaches the area, height falls will help to reduce highs \nmore into the 60s during the day. By Saturday, high pressure will be \ncentered to our west with CAA having pushed highs down into the 50s \nfor most. Saturday night, high pressure will become more directly \ncentered over the region, leading to stronger subsidence and \nradiational cooling with overnight lows dropping well into the 30s. \nThis will likely be the first frost for many places, along with a \nfirst freeze for some as well. \n\nSurface high pressure will remain in place on Sunday with varying \nindications of a shortwave moving in from the west. Continued \nsubsidence and drier air will keep the region dry and mostly clear, \nbut temperatures will remain seasonally cooler through the day. \nAfter the shortwave moves east, subtle height rises with quasi-zonal \nflow will allow for a slight warming trend into the next week. \nOverall, highs should be in the 60s for most with morning lows in \nthe 30s and lower 40s.",
